    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "That makes sense. To go a bit deeper, could you explain the actual chemical mechanism behind why higher pH causes those nutrients to precipitate? Like, what's happening at the molecular level with the ions in the soil solution?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "At a molecular level, elevated pH increases the concentration of hydroxide ions ($OH^-$), which react with cationic micronutrient ions like ferrous ($Fe^{2+}$) and manganous ($Mn^{2+}$) to form insoluble metal hydroxide precipitates rather than keeping them soluble. This electrostatic binding effectively \"locks\" the nutrients into soil particles or low-solubility compounds, drastically reducing their concentration in the soil solution where roots can absorb them. Consequently, as the soil becomes more alkaline, the rate of these precipitation reactions accelerates, rendering these essential metals unavailable to the vine even though they remain physically present in the ground.",
      "source": "target",
